Roster Rules
1. Use the same display_name here as the in-shop card title.
2. Use numeric rarity only so pricing and shop odds stay easy to tune.
3. Keep scaling_summary short; the detailed formulas belong in the prop entry below.
4. implementation_status should change whenever tuning or behavior changes.
5. Add notes only for blockers, edge cases, or special UI/runtime requirements.

Browser roster annotation standard:
Use the existing notes column for quick browser status when a prop has web-specific behavior.
Preferred short tags:
- web:same - effect values match the original version; no card note needed.
- web:visual - effect values match the original version, but browser simplifies VFX or text density.
- web:cap - browser uses a lower actor, projectile, paint, pickup, text, or VFX cap.
- web:fallback - browser replaces a disabled trigger or subsystem with another behavior.
- web:reduced - browser changes an effect number and must show browser-specific values.

Roster browser note examples:
- web:cap pending bullets by headroom, visible bullets 28
- web:same trigger, pulse list updates 24 Hz
- web:visual nearest 8 turrets drawn, all owned turrets simulate
- web:fallback enemy-shot trigger becomes timed pulse
- web:same values match original version, QA dense waves only

Roster browser rules:
1. If notes already contain implementation context, append the browser tag after a semicolon.
2. Use web:same only for high-density or stateful props worth browser QA; omit it for simple stat props.
3. Use web:reduced only when damage, cooldown, radius, chance, duration, or reward value differs.
4. Keep roster browser text short; the detailed prop entry must hold the full cap, fallback, and QA case.
5. Any prop marked web:cap, web:fallback, or web:reduced must have a matching Browser version line in its detailed entry.

