16. Prop Acceptance Criteria
16.1. Functional
All prop ids load from save without migration errors.
No prop purchase can crash the shop UI, even with malformed levels.
All capped props must stop purchasing correctly at max level.
Browser functional:
All implemented prop levels must survive browser refresh through localStorage.
Browser-only feature gates must default to the documented state in the quality payload.
Any prop requiring enemy projectiles must either expose a fallback or document the `enemyshots=1` QA requirement.
Any prop that spawns bullets, coins, particles, text, paint, or turrets must behave correctly when the related browser visible cap is hit.

16.2. Balance
Browser balance:
Browser visual caps must not silently reduce coin, shield, damage, or cooldown value unless the prop is explicitly marked mechanically reduced.
If browser cadence changes perceived timing, compare simulated events per second rather than rendered frames.
If a browser fallback replaces an enemy-shot trigger, its expected proc frequency should stay within +/-15% of the desktop trigger in comparable encounters.
No single economy combo exceeds 2.2x baseline coin generation.

16.3. Performance
Browser performance:
Worst-case prop burst checks must run with `diag=1` and record py_frame heartbeat, browser_gap_ms, pending_bullets, fx_particle_count, dmg_text_count, visible bullet/turret counts, and prop_trigger_counts.
No browser prop burst should starve the event loop long enough to stop diagnostic heartbeat posts.
Late-wave Shrapnel + Explosive + Curing Paint + Turret scenarios must respect visible caps without accumulating unbounded pending entities.
Worst-case combat (projectile + paint + explosion heavy) must hold the 60 FPS target.
No memory leak may occur from persistent status stacks or paint tile maps during a 30-minute session.

16.4. Readability
Browser readability:
At 960x540, browser card text must still show current value, next value, path tag, and any browser_version_note without overlap.
Damage text merging must not hide critical survival information such as shield break, Guardian Charm trigger, or boss telegraph damage.
If paint visuals are skipped in wind/mist browser routes, pause/tooltips must still make it clear that paint effects remain active.
Player can identify prop path from card outline color within 1 second.
Tooltip "Now/Next" text fits in available panel space without clipping.
Damage/event feedback remains available during boss phase transitions.

19. Data Validation Rules (Pre-Commit)
19.1. Numeric validation
Do not allow negative base_price, negative cooldown, or negative duration values.
max_level must be an integer >= 1 or explicit None for stackable or unbounded items.
All percentage fields must use one normalization convention per system (either 0-1 or 0-100, never mixed).

19.2. Cross-field validation
If trigger_type == consumable, require charges or single_use=true.
If the effect includes chain behavior, require chain_depth_cap.
If the effect applies DoT, require tick_interval, duration, and max_stacks.
If the effect applies slow, require boss_slow_modifier.

19.3. UI validation
All props require short tooltip text <= 80 chars and long tooltip text <= 220 chars.
All props require a path color tag for card-border mapping.
All rarity values must map to a defined rarity visual marker count.

19.4. Runtime validation
Every spawned-entity effect must define a performance cap or pool limit.
Every reactive trigger must define an internal cooldown or per-source throttle.
Every saveable prop state must include a default value in run-reset logic.

